 "title": "98: Blind ROP (BROP)",
 "desc": "Goal: Understand Blind Return-Oriented Programming — exploiting a remote service without having the binary. BROP requires only a stack overflow and a crash oracle (the server forks per-connection, so ASLR and canaries stay constant across crashes). The attack proceeds in phases: \n(1) Canary leak: overwrite the canary byte-by-byte, trying all 256 values for each byte. If the server does not crash, the guess is correct. The first byte is always 0x00 (null terminator). \n(2) Stop gadget discovery: scan the text segment for addresses that cause the server to hang (infinite loop, blocking syscall) rather than crash. These are used as safe return targets. \n(3) PLT scanning: identify PLT entries by their unique call+jmp signature. \n(4) Exploitation: chain write@PLT(socket_fd, binary_base, length) to dump the binary over the socket, then build a full ROP chain from the leaked binary.",
 "mode": "asm-quiz",
 "arch": "x86",
 "source": [
  "// Blind ROP (BROP) Attack Phases",
  "",
  "// Phase 1: Canary byte-by-byte leak",
  "// Server forks → canary is constant",
  "for (byte = 0; byte < 4; byte++)",
  "  for (guess = 0; guess < 256; guess++)",
  "    if (send_and_check(guess)) break;",
  "",
  "// Phase 2: Find stop gadgets",
  "// Scan .text for addrs that hang vs crash",
  "for (addr = text_base; ; addr++)",
  "  if (probe(addr) == HANG) save(addr);",
  "",
  "// Phase 3: PLT scanning",
  "// PLT entries: [call resolver][jmp GOT]",
  "// Unique signature: call+jmp pattern",
  "",
  "// Phase 4: Dump binary via write@PLT",
  "write(sock, binary_base, 0x1000);",
  "// Now build full ROP from leaked binary"
 ],
